#%PAM-1.0
# i3lock checks the user's password through Hadron's `login` stack, as
# upstream's own file does. pam_unix runs as the user here, so it reads
# /etc/shadow through the setuid unix_chkpwd helper (Dockerfile `default`
# stage). Without this file PAM falls back to `other`, which denies everything.
#
# Fingerprint (the fingerprint system extension, see
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-24-fingerprint-design.md): i3lock reads the
# password before it runs PAM, so a typed password is checked first and
# unlocks at once; a wrong one, or any single key and Enter, asks for a finger.
# Both modules come only with the extension, and a line that starts with "-"
# is skipped when its module is missing: without the extension this file is
# `auth include login` alone.
-auth      [success=done default=ignore]  pam_isotope_unix.so  nullok
-auth      sufficient   pam_fprintd.so  timeout=10
auth       include      login
